{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789163728,"nonce":"uVGyCnaXU1AY/KgxPJmDu8rNy/E=","title":"haxe_std_downcast dereferences a raw scalar from a Dynamic-typed register","message":"`(Dynamic, _)` in `lower_cast` (compiler/src/ir/mir/expr/casts.rs, the\n`(Some(TypeKind::Dynamic), _)` arm) emits `haxe_std_downcast(value_reg, tag)`\nwithout checking whether `value_reg` already holds a raw primitive. The runtime\n(runtime/src/type_system.rs, `haxe_std_downcast`) does\n`*(value_ptr as *const DynamicValue)`, so an underlying enum-abstract value\n0/1/2 is dereferenced as an address.\n\nREPRO (/tmp/probe/SWABS2.hx, `viaLocalResult`):\n\n    enum abstract Kind(Int) {\n        var A = 0; var B = 1; var C = 2;\n        public function viaLocalResult():String {\n            var s = switch (cast this : Kind) { case A:\"a\"; case B:\"b\"; case C:\"c\"; };\n            return s;\n        }\n    }\n\nwith `var k:Kind = B` gives `SIGSEGV fault=0x1` -- the underlying 1.\n\nThe sibling `(Dynamic, Int)` arm in the same match ALREADY guards this, with a\ncomment naming `cast this` in enum abstract methods. The generic `(Dynamic, _)`\narm has no such guard.\n\nWHY THIS IS SEPARATE FROM a2385ae0: that fixed case-name resolution, and with\nit `viaCast`, `viaThis` and `viaLocal` all return \"b\" correctly. Only the form\nthat routes the switch RESULT through a local still faults, and it faults on\nthe cast, not the patterns.\n\nCONTRIBUTING CAUSE, worth fixing with it: `cast this : Kind` parses as\nECheckType(ECast(this, None), Kind), i.e. TWO nested casts, and the inner one\nis typed Dynamic because the `from_context` filter in\ncompiler/src/tast/ast_lowering/expr/mod.rs accepts only Class|Interface, not\nAbstract. The collapse that would delete that spurious Dynamic hop exists only\non the abstract-method INLINING path (tast_to_hir.rs `inline_expression_deep`),\nso an inlined call is fine and a real call is not. The guard written for this\nexact case at casts.rs requires `inner_expr.ty` to be `TypeKind::Abstract`,\nbut `resolve_this_type` deliberately gives `this` inside an abstract the\nUNDERLYING type -- so that guard is dead code for the case it was written for.\n\nSuggested fix: in the `(Dynamic, _)` arm, if the register already holds a raw\nscalar (I32/I64/U32/F32/F64/Bool), return it rather than calling the runtime --\nmatching what the `(Dynamic, Int)` arm does.","files":null}]}